        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I tried to start writing a comment, but I couldn't find a good way to articulate it, and I assumed it would not be so relevant. Anyways, I can try.\n\nA few weeks ago I briefly experimented with trying to use dir.lua as a base for something like oil.nvim. I would assume that some others have also tried this, or will try it in the future. It actually seemed to work quite okay, at least for some very simple scenarios. I know this is not necessarily the goal of dir.lua, and that something like oil.nvim would probably never be builtin.\n\nHowever, I was really hoping that dir.lua woulf stay very minimal with the keymaps, so that extending it like this would be simpler, so that one would not need to delete keymaps for something like `a` and `o` to work.\n\nAlso, personally, I am not sure I see the gain in a default mapping for something like opening in a split, when it is already very easy to add a keymap (that can also work for a lot of other scenarios), and is also quite east to type: `:vs<CR><CR>`.\n\nOn the other hand, I also know that neovim strides to have good defaults, so I very much understand it is a difficult line to walk, and I would totally respect if others feel differently than I am, and if the default keympas land in neovim.\n\nAnyways, sorry for just downvoting and not trying to explain my reasoning.",
